					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606273909" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1512710220 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Dec 2017 05:17</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3648001">
									

<p>This is the second article I’ve read today where as a reader I’m expected to feel something for a character that is only barely presented through the story.</p>
<p>“This exists and it’s sad/heartwarming/tragic/etc. because it exists” has never been effective for me. The most insight we get into the protagonist is through a brief, surly interview, which makes me completely numb to his romantic plight, which I’m led to believe is the whole reason behind this.</p>
<p>I feel like if you’re going to expect the reader to identify and feel something, you’ve got to do more to forge that connection than what’s presented here. Notes at the end aren’t cutting it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
