															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606273909" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;" >MaliceAforethought</a></span> <span class="odate time_1512722610 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Dec 2017 08:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3648075">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3915 was virtually identical to an ordinary human male except for its incorporeal nature</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If it's still only 'virtually' identical without its anomalous properties, surely that'd mean it had something else abnormal about it. Why not just say 'SCP-3915 was identical [&#8230;] except for its incorporeal nature'?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;a gift from a friend&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do not like this. This, combined with the &quot;[DESTROYED]&quot; later on, just feels like a lazy excuse to avoid having to convey certain parts of the story. That may just be me, but it's quite a large downside to an otherwise adequate article. Sorry :/</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a written document that read 'gone to do what should have been done years ago'</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Spooky ghost leaves cryptic clues that are still blindingly obvious, making me wonder what the point of making it an un-capitalised sentence fragment even was.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>okay. i know you're not coast guard. it's fine.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again with this. Why does this person go into the trouble of dotting capital Is incorrectly, but still uses apostrophes in the correct places. It seems like you're making it more 'realistic' by making this person seem stupid (in a weirdly specific way).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall I agree with Kalinin above: there's nothing that makes me feel for the character, and the way the letters were written distracted me from their contents. The idea doesn't seem distinct from any other incorporeal humanoids we have on the site, and I never found myself 'involved' with the narrative. The core ideas are good, but I think you could come up with a much deeper backstory than &quot;we fought&quot; and a much better anomaly than 'ghost (actually not a ghost)'.</p>
<p>Reluctant downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
